"The opening verses of Surah Fussilat state that the Quran is a book whose verses have been clearly explained in detail (tafsil). The polytheists refused to listen to the Prophet, asserting that their hearts were veiled and their ears were heavy. According to a narration, Abu Jahl placed a curtain between himself and the Prophet; however, the following day seventy people came asking him to explain Islam, and they embraced Islam.\n\nIn the verse concerning the creation of the earth in two days, the word 'yawm' denotes time in an absolute sense. While some commentators interpret these two days as the separation of the earth from the heaven and the leveling of the earth, others maintain that they point to the turning of the seasons. Within four days, the mountains, blessings, and sustenance were ordained. These four days, together with the preceding two days, constitute six days, which aligns with the six days of creation mentioned in other verses.\n\nThe creation of the heaven while it was smoke and the command given to the earth and the heaven to 'come, willingly or unwillingly,' signifies their obedience. This command is to be understood as natural laws submitting to the divine will. The heavens were arranged into seven tiers in two days, and the nearest heaven was adorned with stars and safeguarded. Thereafter, the polytheists are warned through the reminder of the destruction of the peoples of 'Ad and Thamud.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, the ears, eyes, and skins of the disbelievers will bear witness against them. Their skins will say that Allah made them speak. The disbelievers committed sins assuming that Allah would not know. Companions from among the devils were set over them, and these companions made their evil deeds seem fair to them. Thus, the decree of punishment was fulfilled against them.\n\nThe disbelievers attempted to drown out the Quran by making noise while it was being recited. Allah will make them taste a severe punishment and will requite them for the worst of their deeds. As for those who say, 'Our Lord is Allah,' and then remain steadfast, the angels descend upon them, telling them not to fear and giving them the glad tidings of Paradise. The angels are their allies in this worldly life and in the Hereafter. This is hospitality from Allah, the Oft-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.\n\nWho is better in speech than one who invites to Allah, performs righteous deeds, and says, 'I am of the Muslims'? Evil must be repelled in the best possible manner so that even enmity may turn into friendship. None attain this maturity except those who show patience. The night and the day, the sun and the moon are signs of Allah's existence. Prostration must be made only to Allah, who created them. Just as Allah brings the dead earth back to life, He will likewise resurrect the dead.\n\nThose who deny the Quran when it comes to them will surely suffer their punishment. It is a profoundly precious book; falsehood cannot approach it from before it or from behind it. It has been revealed by Allah, the All-Wise, the Praiseworthy. Nothing is said to our Prophet except what was said to the messengers before him. Your Lord is the possessor of both forgiveness and painful punishment. In response to those who object to the Quran being in Arabic, it is stated that even if it had been revealed in a foreign tongue, they would still have objected. The Quran is guidance and healing for those who believe; for those who disbelieve, there is a heaviness in their ears, and it is blindness to them. It is as if they are being called from a distant place and do not hear.\n\nInviting to Allah is the most beautiful of speech. There are two conditions for this invitation: the inviter must himself perform righteous deeds and be a Muslim in true sincerity. The one who invites must say, 'I am of the Muslims,' call to good, and ensure that their deeds are in harmony with their call. Evil and good are not equal; the finest invitation is to repay evil with good. When this is done, even enmity becomes like a close relative. This disposition is granted only to those who are patient and possess a great portion of good. If a prompting from Satan should arise, one must immediately seek refuge in Allah.\n\nThe night and the day, the sun and the moon are among the proofs of Allah's existence and power. Prostration should not be made to them, but rather to Allah who created them. There are juridical rulings concerning the verse of prostration (sajdah): while Imam al-Shafi'i states that prostration should be performed at verse 41:37, according to Imam al-A'zam Abu Hanifa, it is performed at 41:38. The revival of the earth with rain after drought also demonstrates Allah's power; it is proof that He will also revive the dead.\n\nThose who deviate (ilhad) regarding Our verses—that is, those who stray from righteousness, disbelieve, and distort—will be cast into the Fire. These are the ones who deny the Quran. The Quran is an unassailable (aziz) and praiseworthy (hamid) book; falsehood cannot approach it. Nothing was said to our Prophet except what had been said to the prophets before him. The disbelievers criticized the Quran for being in Arabic. Yet the Quran is guidance and healing for those who believe, whereas the disbelievers cannot benefit from it.\n\nMoses was also given the Torah, and dispute arose concerning it. Had a prior word not gone forth from your Lord delaying the punishment, judgment would have been decreed between them immediately. They are in grave doubt concerning the Quran. Whoever does good does so for the benefit of their own soul, and whoever does evil does so against it. Your Lord is never unjust to His servants.\n\nKnowledge of the Hour belongs exclusively to Allah. No being besides Him possesses this knowledge. On that Day, when Allah questions them concerning the partners they associated with Him, they will say, 'None of us bears witness to that.' The things they used to worship previously will vanish from them, and they will find no place of escape. Man never tires of asking for good, but when evil touches him, he falls into despair. When he is made to taste mercy, he becomes arrogant and denies the Hereafter. Allah will surely inform the disbelievers of what they did and will make them taste a heavy punishment. When favor is bestowed upon man, he turns away; but when evil touches him, he prays profusely. Ask our Prophet: If the Quran has indeed come from Allah and yet you have denied it, who could be further astray?\n\nWe will show the disbelievers Our signs both on the horizons and within their own souls until it becomes completely manifest that the Quran is the truth. It is sufficient that your Lord is a witness over all things. Indeed, they are in doubt about the meeting with their Lord. Yet Allah encompasses all things in His knowledge and power. They cannot escape His punishment. Meeting Allah is true and inevitable."